# axtun configuration.
#
# Two things live here: which station reaches which addresses, and what is
# allowed out of the radio.
#
# Copy to /etc/axcall/axtun for everyone, or ~/.config/axcall/axtun for
# yourself; a route in the per-user file replaces one for the same range in the
# system file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Routes
#
# A TUN device is NOARP: the kernel hands the packet over and never asks who
# owns the address on this link. So this table is not an optimisation, it is
# the whole of how a packet finds a station, and it is static on purpose.
# Deciding at run time which transmitter to key, based on something a stranger
# said on the air, is not a decision to hand to a discovery protocol.
#
# Longest prefix wins, as in any routing table: a host route beats the subnet
# it sits in, and the subnet beats the default.
#
# "via" gives a digipeater path, up to eight calls, comma separated with no
# spaces. Each one costs seven bytes of every frame and a whole extra
# transmission, so two is already a long path for IP. Without it, nothing
# behind a digi is reachable at all.
#
# An address heard on the air but not listed here is remembered for an hour,
# so a station that ARPs for us becomes reachable without being written down.
# Anything listed here wins over anything heard.

# range                callsign     path
#route 44.131.20.2      PN0TST
#route 44.131.91.0/24   GB7RDG-1    via WIDE1-1
#route default          GB7RDG-1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What may go out
#
# Put an unfiltered IP interface on a shared radio channel and the machine will
# transmit, unasked and forever: mDNS, LLMNR, SSDP, NetBIOS, DHCP renewals,
# IPv6 router solicitations, NTP. Nobody requested any of it, all of it goes
# out under the operator's callsign, and on a channel where one full frame
# takes over a second at 1200 baud it is not harmless background noise.
#
#   allow|deny <protocol> [to <range>] [port <n[-m]>]
#
# Rules are tried in order and the first match decides. Anything no rule
# matches does not go out. The protocol is icmp, tcp, udp, a number, or "any";
# the destination defaults to any; only tcp and udp rules can name a port.
#
# Two things no ordinary rule can let through. A multicast, broadcast or
# link-local destination needs a rule that names its range explicitly, because
# a wildcard rule was written by somebody thinking about unicast. And anything
# that is not IPv4 never reaches the filter, because a packet whose shape is
# not understood cannot be judged.
#
# With no allow or deny lines at all, a default policy applies: ping, TCP and
# UDP to a unicast address, with the loud ports taken back out. The first time
# each kind of packet is dropped, axtun says so on standard error, which is how
# to find out what to add here.

#allow icmp
#allow tcp to 44.0.0.0/8
#allow udp to 44.0.0.0/8 port 123
#deny  tcp to 44.0.0.0/8 port 445
